  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nThe weather pattern will shift over the weekend into early next week \nas the subtropical ridge once again becomes the dominant weather \nfeature across the Southwestern U.S. The Pacific trough will \nsomewhat stick around into Saturday, but it is shown to quickly lift \nto the north by Sunday as the ridge moves back in from the east. \nForecast H5 heights rise from 588-590dm on Friday to 591-594dm \nSaturday before peaking around 594-596dm during the first half of \nnext week. \n\nThe dry air and increasing subsidence this weekend should keep all \nbut a few eastern Arizona high terrain showers and storms from \ndeveloping. Temperatures will warm up quickly over the weekend with \nthe expansion of the ridge with NBM forecast highs reaching 103-106 \ndegrees Saturday and 106-109 degrees Sunday. The drop in moisture \nwill at least push surface dew points well into the 50s this weekend \nto as low as the upper 40s early next week. This will help to keep \novernight lows close to normals through the weekend before we heat \nup further next week. Ensembles eventually show the ridge center \nshifting directly over Arizona by around Monday, likely staying in \nplace for a few days. The increased subsidence from the ridge with \nno meaningful moisture return happening early next week should keep \nlower desert PoPs below 10% to at most 10-20% over the far eastern \nArizona high terrain.\n\nAs the modestly strong (90-97th percentile of climatology) ridge \nsettles over Arizona during the first half of next week, \ntemperatures are likely to heat up enough to result in Major \nHeatRisk across portions of the lower deserts. The highest \nlikelihood for Major HeatRisk looks to impact the Phoenix metro with \ndaytime highs around 110 degrees and lows around 90 degrees starting \nMonday, but the more rural lower desert areas should experience \nsimilar daytime highs as well. Due to the expectation of Major \nHeatRisk, we have issued an Extreme Heat Watch for Maricopa County, \nto include the Phoenix Metro, and Pinal County, valid from Monday \nthrough Wednesday",
